Pavements slippery, people sneezing;  
Lords in ermine, beggars freezing;  
Titled gluttons carving dainties,  
Genius starving in a garret.  

Lofty mansions, warm and spacious;  
Courtiers cringing and voracious;  
Misers scarcely heed the wretched;  
Gallant soldiers fight and bleed.  

Wives who laugh at passive spouses;  
Theatres and meeting-houses;  
Balls where simpering misses languish;  
Hospitals with groans of anguish.  

Arts and sciences bewailing;  
Commerce drooping, credit failing;  
Placemen mocking loyal subjects;  
Separations, royal weddings.  

Authors who cannot earn a dinner;  
Many a subtle rogue a winner;  
Fugitives who shelter seek;  
Misers hoarding, tradesmen breaking.  

Taste and talent both deserted;  
All the laws of truth perverted;  
Arrogance above merit soaring;  
Merit silently deploring.  

Ladies gambling night and morning;  
Fools the works of genius scorning;  
Ancient dames for girls mistaken;  
Youthful damsels quite forsaken.  

Some delighting in their luxury;  
More inclined to talk than fighting;  
Aged lovers, beaux decrepit;  
Lordlings empty and insipid.  

Poets, painters, and musicians;  
Lawyers, doctors, politicians;  
Pamphlets, newspapers, and odes,  
Seeking fame by different roads.  

Gallant souls with empty purses;  
Generals fit for little but nurses;  
Schoolboys, smitten with martial spirit,  
Taking the place of veteran merit.  

Honest men who cannot gain positions;  
Knaves displaying unblushing faces;  
Ruin hastened, peace retarded;  
Candor spurned and art rewarded.

outputs.L2:
Icy streets underfoot, sneezes filling the air;  
Peers wrapped in fur while paupers shiver.  
Noble gourmands slice rich delicacies,  
As brilliance goes hungry beneath an attic roof.

Grand houses stand roomy and well heated;  
Greedy courtiers bow and flatter.  
The miser barely notices those in misery,  
While courageous troops struggle and spill blood.

Women ridicule husbands who meekly endure them;  
Playhouses and houses of worship draw their crowds.  
At dances, coy young ladies pine and pose;  
In infirmaries, sufferers cry out in pain.

The arts and learned disciplines lament;  
Trade declines and confidence collapses.  
Officeholders jeer at faithful citizens;  
There are partings, and marriages among royalty.

Writers cannot make enough to buy a meal,  
Yet many a crafty villain prospers.  
Runaways search for a safe refuge;  
The stingy pile up wealth as merchants fail.

Refinement and ability are wholly neglected;  
Every principle of honesty is twisted.  
Conceit rises above genuine worth,  
While worth grieves without complaint.

Women of rank gamble from dawn until night;  
Idiots dismiss the creations of gifted minds.  
Elderly matrons are taken for maidens,  
While actual young women are ignored.

A few surrender themselves to extravagance;  
Many boast of combat more than they engage in it.  
Suitors are aged, and dandies worn out;  
Petty nobles are hollow and dull.

Poets, artists, composers,  
Attorneys, physicians, and statesmen—  
Through essays, journals, and verse,  
Each pursues renown along a separate path.

Brave spirits possess not a coin;  
Commanders are scarcely capable of leaving the nursery.  
Boys still at school, seized by dreams of war,  
Are preferred to seasoned veterans of proven worth.

Decent men are denied public positions;  
Scoundrels display themselves without shame.  
Disaster is accelerated and peace postponed;  
Sincerity is rejected, while cunning receives the prize.

