### JANUARY, 1795.

Pavement slippery, people sneezing,  
Lords in ermine, beggars freezing;  
Titled gluttons dainties carving,  
Genius in a garret starving.

Lofty mansions, warm and spacious;  
Courtiers cringing and voracious;  
Misers scarce the wretched heeding;  
Gallant soldiers fighting, bleeding.

### January

Pavement slipp’ry, people sneezing,  
Lords in ermine, beggars freezing;  
Titled beauties skipping, writhing,  
The Fleet Ditch with mud overbrimming;  
Horses falling, cabmen swearing,  
Hawkers shouting, beggars praying;  
Dandies lounging, ladies bustling,  
Truant schoolboys fighting, hustling;  
Newsmen lying, tradesmen cheating,  
Fiddlers scraping, soldiers beating;  
If to this you add the weather,  
January’s brought together.

**The Town’s Account**

Awake, my Muse! let London’s ledger show  
What gilded heights from muddy channels grow;  
Where Belgrave’s plate reflects the morning sun,  
And Shoreditch starves ere Mayfair’s meal be done.  

Here lords, by acres fed, complain of rent;  
There babes lack bread, that peers may scent their scent.  
The coach flies on, bespatt’ring want with mire,  
Whilst frozen fingers warm at painted fire.  

Yon widow pawns the blanket from her bed;  
Yon duke buys votes, and calls the nation fed.  
The beadle, big with parish power and ale,  
Drives hunger forth, then sups within the gaol.  

At Court, each bow conceals a private rate;  
A smile gains office, and a shrug the State.  
Who serves the Crown?—the rogue whose purse hath weight;  
Who serves the realm?—the fool that comes too late.  

A cousin’s wit commands the Treasury key;  
A patriot’s worth is—what his friends agree.  
The placeman cries, “Reform!” with noble heat,  
Then adds three sinecures and keeps his seat.  

In Commons, thunder rolls from bench to bench;  
’Tis bought with burgundy, and paid in French.  
The borough, snug where scarce ten chimneys smoke,  
Sends two grave tongues to vote beneath the yoke;  

Whilst Manchester, with thousand hammers loud,  
Must beg one whisper for its sweating crowd.  
“Free English hearts!” the minister proclaims,  
Then hires informers, spies on clubs, and names.  

At White’s, a fortune trembles on a die;  
Young Midas laughs, though all his lands must fly.  
A hazard cast consumes th’ ancestral plain;  
The oaks become a groom’s ill-gotten gain.  

The duchess stakes her diamonds, fan, and fame;  
The sharper bows—and wins them all the same.  
At Brooks’s, patriots wager war or peace,  
And toast mankind till Burgundy shall cease.  

The alley roars: “South Sea! New gold! Peru!”  
Each gull sells truth to purchase what ne’er grew.  
A bubble swells; the citizen grows great—  
At noon a prince, at four a bankrupt’s mate.  

The broker, sleek, interprets every lie;  
If armies bleed, his Consols mount the sky.  
He counts each volley by the market’s tone,  
And builds his villa out of soldiers’ bone.  

From Wapping’s masts the merchant banners stream;  
Tea, sugar, silk fulfil the patriot’s dream.  
Ask not what chained hands cut the cane so white;  
The cup looks clean—why question whence the blight?  

The East sends spice, the West its crimson gain;  
The ledger perfumes avarice and pain.  
A sermon blesses commerce, fleet, and throne;  
The preacher’s cassock hath a planter’s loan.  

Now drums beat glory through the crowded Strand;  
“Fight, lads, for liberty—in foreign land!”  
The press-gang proves how freely Britons sail;  
A broken skull supplies what speeches fail.  

The general, safe, describes the glorious field;  
The widow learns what triumphs truly yield.  
For him, a star; for her, a parish crust;  
For him, bronze marble; him she loved—red dust.  

At Drury Lane, a king in pasteboard reigns;  
The pit applauds his counterfeit campaigns.  
A painted forest shakes at thunder’s call,  
More moving than the real woods we maul.  

The poet starves, but sings Mæcenas’ grace;  
The patron yawns, then asks the author’s place.  
The painter flatters every bloated chin,  
And shades out gout as carefully as sin.  

Italian warblers trill at vast expense,  
Whilst English sense wants supper, shoes, and sense.  
A connoisseur, who scarce can spell his name,  
Buys cracked old gods and manufactures fame.  

In Grub Street garrets, ink and hunger strive;  
The bookseller grows fat, the wits contrive.  
One pens for Church, one writes the Church accurst;  
Both praise the side whose guinea reaches first.  

Fleet Street pours scandal piping from the press:  
A lord’s new vice, a lady’s old undress.  
To-day the mob cries, “Hang him!” at the news;  
To-morrow, “Crown him!”—if he change his views.  

At Tyburn, justice mounts the triple tree;  
The poor thief swings, the rich thief takes his fee.  
Steal bread—thy neck must answer for the crime;  
Steal provinces—thy statue stands in time.  

The judge condemns, then dines on turtle soup;  
Law spreads her net, but gold slips through each loop.  
The debtor rots where fever salts the air;  
His creditor buys pews, and kneels in prayer.  

Gin-lanes run bright where hope hath ceased to run;  
The mother drinks, the infant sees no sun.  
Yet saints in satin, warm with righteous zeal,  
Debate if starving folk deserve a meal.  

The city feast heaps venison to the roof;  
Outside, gaunt Want presents her living proof.  
“Work!” cries the alderman, with greasy breath,  
To him whose fourteen hours have earn’d him death.  

Thus Trade grows rich, and traders richlier dressed;  
The loom grows swift, the weaver lacks a vest.  
The mines yield fire, the miner coughs it back;  
The nation shines—its children all grow black.  

O London! monstrous mart of mud and gold,  
Where all is purchas’d, conscience bought and sold;  
Where Freedom struts in chains of newest mode,  
And Charity takes care to know the code;  

Where kings need cash, and cash creates a peer;  
Where Truth speaks low, lest Power chance to hear;  
Where every knave calls private plunder “trade,”  
And every tax is “for the public” made.  

Still bells ring out, and loyal bonfires blaze;  
The hungry cheer, instructed whom to praise.  
So rolls the Town, by fraud and fashion driven—  
Too proud for earth, too profitable for Heaven.
